ative work of it, you'd generally have to release your product's source under GPL-3.0 too. Shipping binaries, an installer, a desktop or mobile app, or an on-prem or embedded build all count as distribution.\n- Running it only on your own servers (SaaS) usually doesn't trigger GPL-3.0's source obligations. That's a different story if it were AGPL, so check the actual license text.\n- GPL-3.0 also has anti-tivoization and patent-license terms. These make it hard to ship in locked-down hardware or app-store contexts.\n\nYour options:\n1. Replace it with a permissively licensed alternative (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0).\n2. Ask the author or copyright holder for a commercial or dual license.\n3. Isolate it as a separate program that you call across a process boundary. This is legally subtle, so get counsel's sign-off before relying on it.\n4. Drop the feature that needs it.\n\n**The others**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0) and `left-pad` (MIT) are fine for closed source. You still need to ship their license texts. For Apache-2.0, that includes any NOTICE file contents, and you must keep the copyright notices.\n\n**Caveats**\n- I only have the license labels written in `deps.txt`. I haven't checked them against the real packages. Confirm `somelib`'s license on its repo or package metadata, since labels can be wrong, or it may be dual-licensed.\n- The file lists only top-level dependencies. Transitive dependencies can bring in their own licenses, including more GPL or AGPL. Run a license scanner on the full resolved tree.\n- Vendored code isn't covered by the file either. I found no LICENSE or NOTICE files in the repo. If you ship third-party code, you'll need to create an attribution or NOTICE file.\n- I'm not a lawyer. Since this affects a release, have legal review the `somelib` decision.\n\nI can check for transitive dependencies or draft a third-party notices file if that would help.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}